Chapter: HalFlashLinear
  cites Foreword chapter Board

 The flash bank lifecycle, done right. A bank is unlocked once, two
 pages are programmed into it, and it is sealed once, threading the
 linear handle through every step.

 The seal returns 0, and that is the assertion: a bank reports its own
 soundness at seal time. Every page write polled the controller's busy
 flag and found it ready, so no write was lost and the fault count is
 zero. A negative answer would be the count of pages whose busy-wait ran
 out of fuel.

 The register writes are real memory-mapped stores (the base is inside
 mapped RAM), so this exercises the honest MMIO path, not a stub.

 The four adversarial counterparts are errors/hal-flash-leak,
 errors/hal-flash-double-seal, errors/hal-flash-use-after-seal, and
 errors/flash-launder-mmio -- the last being the capability gate itself:
 Device.Mmio is the mechanism and cannot buy the authority.

Section: Body
  stage : Integer, Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] Integer
  stage (base) (page) = act
    b0 <- flash-open-bank base
    b1 <- flash-write-page b0 page [72, 73, 74, 75]
    b2 <- flash-write-page b1 (page + 4) [76, 77]
    flash-seal-bank b2
  end

  opening : [Console, Flash, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    r <- stage 536875008 536879104
    print-line-uni ("flash lifecycle ok: " & show r)
  end
